# ---- Other versions of the changes (teacher view only) ----
# Drafted from memory of the usual printed and jam-session changes, not copied
# from an edition: check each against your own books before trusting it.

{start_of_version: Songbook}
source: the plain changes most singalong and easy-piano books print (unverified)
Tonic for two bars, dominant for two, then dominant and its ii swapping under "a beautiful sight, we're happy tonight" and II7 V7 I to finish.
The bridge is the same eight bars as written without the colour: I V7 I in G, then in Bb, and a Bb7 to come home. Many guitar charts play IV for the V7 there (G C G, Bb Eb Bb).
The 1934 sheet is in Eb, in cut time, and was played as a foxtrot.
Verse, Interlude:
| Eb | % | Bb7 | % |
| Bb7 Fm | Bb7 Fm | F7 Bb7 | Eb |
Bridge:
| G D7 | G | G D7 | G |
| Bb F7 | Bb | C7 F7 | Bb7 |
Coda:
| F7 Bb7 | Eb | half F7 | Bb7 | Eb | % |
{end_of_version}

{start_of_version: Jazz ii-V}
style: swing
source: jam-session changes (unverified); the I VI7 ii V turnaround is as taught by PianoGroove
Every dominant gets its ii in front of it. Ebmaj7 C7b9 Fm7 Bb7 opens each verse, and the bridge approaches G and then Bb through their own ii-V.
The last bar of the bridge turns back with Fm7 Bb7.
Verse, Interlude:
| Ebmaj7 | Ebmaj7 C7b9 | Fm7 | Bb7 |
| Fm7 Bb7 | Fm7 Bb7 | F9 Bb7 | Eb6 |
Bridge:
| Gmaj7 . Am7 D7 | Gmaj7 | Gmaj7 . Am7 D7 | Gmaj7 |
| Bbmaj7 . Cm7 F7 | Bbmaj7 | C7 F7 | Fm7 Bb7 |
Coda:
| F9 Bb7 | Eb6 | half F9 | Bb7 | Eb6 | % |
{end_of_version}
